    "scenario": "llm-llm",
    "source": "azure",
    "why": "THE ARRIVAL PROCESS, FOR ONCE. Every other tier in this study -- T6 included -- regenerates arrivals as Poisson at a requested rate, so offered load stays a controlled variable. That is defensible and it is what the Splitwise authors did for cluster sizing, but it means NO tier anywhere replays a production arrival process, and the findings report has to say so. T20 replays the real inter-arrival gaps from a contiguous window of AzureLLMInferenceTrace_conv.csv. WHAT TO EXPECT, registered before the run: not much, and that is the point. The conversation trace measures at an inter-arrival CV of 1.057-1.094, which is close to Poisson, and no gap in it exceeds ~4 s. So if the T6 result holds here, the Poisson substitution used everywhere else is VINDICATED for this corpus, and that is a stronger statement than any single tier's effect size. If it does NOT hold, every tier in the study inherits a caveat. Either way the answer is worth one run. NOTE the rate argument no longer controls offered load: real gaps set it, and the achieved rate was 7.41/s against 6.00 requested when the trace was generated."
  },
